CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, functionally incapacitating physical exhaustion, reactive tachycardia (HR 98), and mild hypotension (BP 105/65) directly resulting from profound occupational overexertion and invisible labor. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critically necessary to rule out an acute organic collapse, such as severe post-viral fatigue or an endocrine crisis, and to provide immediate medical certification to halt the unsustainable work cycle. Delaying care until routine hours would have forced the patient to attempt to log on to work, risking a more severe physiological or psychiatric breakdown, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Issued a strict 7-day medical certificate for immediate stress leave. Advised the usual GP to review documented asymmetry in professional service commitments and seek psychometric evidence of occupational burnout.
Positive Behavioral Change: To mitigate invisible labor burnout, implement a strict "auto-decline protocol" for all non-core tasks: draft a single, polite template declining new committee or mentorship requests; utilizing this template reduces the cognitive load of saying no and physically protects recovery time. To manage the physical exhaustion, enforce a 48-hour "digital blackout": completely power down all work devices and store them in another room; removing the visual and auditory cues of work prevents the habitual checking that spikes adrenaline.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an executive coach specializing in minority tax and boundary setting.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 5 days to assess physical recovery, review blood tests, and plan a sustainable return-to-work strategy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing sudden chest pain, extreme dizziness resulting in fainting, or if the severe exhaustion transitions into active thoughts of self-harm.

